1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
A wild wolf can hear you before you know it is there. It catches your scent moving through the trees. It knows your

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:15,000
direction. It may have watched you cross an opening, noticed that you are alone, and followed quietly enough that your entire understanding of the encounter

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
begins several minutes late. Then the wolf leaves. This is the part that feels wrong. A grey wolf is large enough to

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
injure a human, fast enough to catch one, and equipped with teeth designed to hold struggling animals. Wolves

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,000
cooperate. They kill deer, elk, moose, and occasionally bison. Animals that make an unarmed person look structurally

6
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:46,000
unfinished. Yet, millions of people have walked, hunted, camped, and worked inside wolf country without being

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,000
attacked. Not because wolves are gentle, not because humans taste bad, and not because every wolf has signed a private

8
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:02,000
agreement recognizing us as the main character. Most wild wolves avoid us because attacking a human is usually a terrible decision. We do not behave like

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:10,000
their normal prey. We are difficult to evaluate, capable of sudden retaliation and connected to a species that has spent centuries making wolves regret

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:19,000
being noticed. Wild wolves generally remain wary of people and official park guidance describes attacks as exceptionally uncommon. The wolf is not

11
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:28,000
looking at a meal and choosing mercy. It is looking at risk. Let's go through it properly. Start with the correction hidden inside the question. Wild wolves

12
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,000
can attack humans. They have done so historically and rare verified attacks still occur. Some have been predatory.

13
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Some were defensive or provoked. Many of the most dramatic historical incidents involved rabies, a disease capable of

14
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:52,000
destroying normal weariness and producing abnormal aggression. Any explanation claiming wolves never attack people is comforting, simple, and false.

15
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:00,000
The real question is why attacks remain so uncommon compared with the number of opportunities. Wolves overlap with people across enormous regions of North

16
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,000
America, Europe, and Asia. They cross roads, pass farms, move near settlements, and sometimes travel

17
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:16,000
through landscapes so heavily modified by humans that avoiding every human structure would require leaving the continent. They encounter our scent

18
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,000
constantly. They usually choose distance. That choice begins with what a wolf is actually built to hunt. Wolves

19
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:31,000
are cooperative pursuit predators, but that description can make them sound more unstoppable than they are. A wolf does not possess a cat's retractable

20
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:38,000
claws or forlims specialized for grappling. It usually subdues large prey with repeated bites, group pressure,

21
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
exhaustion, and the discovery of one vulnerable animal inside a herd. Discovery is the important part.

22
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Yellowstone research shows that wolves test far more elk than they successfully kill. They often target calves, old

23
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:59,000
individuals, debilitated animals, or prey placed at a disadvantage by deep snow, poor footing, or terrain. Even

24
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:06,000
with cooperation, wolf hunting success is frequently low. The pack is not an elite tactical unit that selects any

25
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:14,000
body on the landscape and deletes it. It searches for the animal whose defenses are already beginning to fail. That is why a healthy adult elk can be more

26
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:22,000
dangerous to a wolf than the phrase prey animal suggests. Elk kick, moose strike with long legs. Bison turn, group

27
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:31,000
together, and defend calves. One broken jaw, damaged leg, or punctured abdomen can leave a wolf unable to feed.

28
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Yellowstone wolves prefer elk over bison, partly because bison are formidable, cooperative defenders, and larger packs are generally needed to

29
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:47,000
kill them. Predators do not receive medical leave. A failed hunt can cost energy. An injury can cost every future

30
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:55,000
hunt. So, wolves evaluate. They approach, watch, test movement, pressure groups, and abandon many attempts when

31
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:03,000
the prey remains strong or the geometry becomes bad. Their success depends not only on what they can kill, but on what

32
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:12,000
they can kill without paying too much for it. Now, place a human inside that calculation. We are not physically impressive by large mammal standards. We

33
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:20,000
have no horns. Our skin is thin. Our natural bite is mainly useful for making dentists expensive. A lone unarmed

34
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:29,000
person is absolutely vulnerable to a determined wolf. But humans do not resemble the animals wolves usually hunt. We stand upright. We turn and face

35
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:37,000
danger. We use our front limbs constantly. We make direct irregular movements. We carry objects. We travel

36
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:44,000
with dogs, tools, fire, vehicles, and other humans who may be hidden nearby.

37
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Most importantly, wolves have learned that the small upright animal can cause damage from a distance. A deer has never

38
00:04:51,000 --> 00:05:00,000
fired a warning shot. A moose has never returned the next morning with several relatives, tracking equipment, and a government form. From the wolf's

39
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:09,000
perspective, the human body is only the visible portion of the threat. That lesson has been repeated for a very long time. Humans and wolves have competed

40
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,000
over prey, livestock, carcasses, and territory across much of the northern hemisphere. People trapped wolves,

41
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:23,000
poisoned them, shot them, destroyed dens, killed pups, and organized eradication campaigns powerful enough to

42
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:31,000
remove wolves from enormous parts of their former range. Wolves were nearly eliminated from much of their historical North American range through sustained

43
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:40,000
human persecution. The surviving populations did not need to understand history. They needed to respond correctly to human cues. A wolf that

44
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:48,000
approached people boldly was more likely to be killed. A wolf that moved away, traveled at quieter times, used cover,

45
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:53,000
and kept distance had a better chance of surviving long enough to reproduce.

46
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Individual experience reinforced the pattern, while development and learning from other wolves could shape how younger animals navigated human

47
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:09,000
landscapes. Wolf behavior reflects both inherited tendencies and individual and social experience. The result was not

48
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:17,000
perfect genetically programmed terror of every person. It was a deep tendency toward caution. Human presence now

49
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:26,000
shapes wolf movement even where wolves are legally protected. Tracking research across United States national parks found consistently negative wolf

50
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:33,000
responses to human footprint, while other work shows wolves avoiding areas of high human influence. The irony is

51
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:42,000
that wolves can live surprisingly close to people while remaining almost invisible to them. Distance does not always mean wilderness. Sometimes it

52
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:49,000
means arriving after we leave. This is why hikers can spend years in wolf habitat without seeing one. While wolves

53
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:57,000
repeatedly detect the hikers, human vision requires the animal to step into view. A wolf can identify our smell,

54
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:05,000
sound, path, and recent presence without introducing itself. You think there was no encounter. The wolf made the decision

55
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:12,000
before you arrived. There is another reason ordinary encounters are easy to misread. Wolves investigate. A wolf may

56
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:21,000
stand and watch because it is gathering information, not because it has reached the final stage of a hunt. It may move parallel to a trail, appear again

57
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:29,000
farther ahead, or follow at a distance while deciding what a person, dog, vehicle, or unfamiliar smell means. To

58
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:36,000
the human nervous system, being followed by a wolf and being hunted by a wolf feel almost identical. The wolf's

59
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:43,000
behavior can be different. Predatory attention becomes more concerning when the animal repeatedly closes distance,

60
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:51,000
approaches directly, ignores attempts to frighten it, circles closer, focuses on a child or dog, or behaves with the

61
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:56,000
persistence expected from an animal that no longer treats humans as dangerous.

62
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:03,000
One distant observation is not the same as a fearless approach. Wildlife managers focus strongly on repeated close approaches and advanced

63
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:11,000
habituation when assessing risk. Context is doing the work again. Howling creates similar confusion. A wolf howl is not

64
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:19,000
normally an announcement that an attack team has been activated. Wolves howl to maintain contact, locate pack members,

65
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:24,000
and advertise territory across distances where visual communication is useless.

66
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:32,000
Experimental research has shown howling responses varying with pack composition, season, and territorial behavior. A

67
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:39,000
reply may come from animals several kilometers away. The sound feels close because low frequencies travel. The

68
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:48,000
wolves may not be. Humans hear several voices answering in darkness. And imagine a circle tightening. The pack may simply be completing the wilderness

69
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,000
equivalent of a family group chat with significantly better acoustics.

70
00:08:52,000 --> 00:09:01,000
This matters because fear encourages bad decisions. A person who panics may run, separate from companions, abandon useful

71
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:09,000
equipment, or allow a dog to bolt. The wolf that had been communicating with its family now watches the strange upright animal create an entirely new

72
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:16,000
situation. Calm behavior preserves information. Face the animal. Watch what

73
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:24,000
it actually does. Give it a clear route away. A wolf that wants distance often takes the route immediately. A wolf that

74
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:33,000
continues approaching after being recognized, confronted, and given space is communicating something more unusual and should be treated seriously.

75
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:40,000
The difference between cautious observation and dangerous boldness is not always obvious from one moment.

76
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:49,000
Wildlife managers therefore look for patterns. Has the same wolf entered camps repeatedly? Has it approached people in daylight? Does it remain after

77
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:57,000
shouting, clapping, or hazing? Has it received food? Is it targeting dogs or children? One close photograph cannot

78
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:04,000
answer those questions. Repeated behavior can. This is why filming a fearless wolf while celebrating how

79
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:13,000
comfortable it is around people may document the beginning of a management problem rather than a magical connection with nature. Fear, however, is not one

80
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:20,000
simple reaction. A wolf fleeing at the first hint of a person, and a wolf calmly crossing a road 200 meters away

81
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:26,000
may both be behaving cautiously. Animals living in human dominated landscapes must tolerate some disturbance or lose

82
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:34,000
access to most of their habitat. By the way, if you want to go even deeper into the secret lives of ancient humans, I

83
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:42,000
just launched a full course called The Secret Lives of Ancient Humans. 10 modules, three plus hours of content

84
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:50,000
covering everything we never talk about on YouTube. What they feared, how they communicated, what hunted them, and much

85
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:56,000
more, plus an exclusive PDF guide included. Link in the description. See you inside.

86
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:03,000
A wolf can become accustomed to vehicles, distant buildings, or people who consistently remain far away without

87
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:11,000
becoming dangerous. This is ordinary habituation, a reduced response to a repeated stimulus that has produced no

88
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:19,000
meaningful consequence. The problem begins when the distance keeps shrinking. A wolf repeatedly exposed to people at close range may learn that

89
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:27,000
humans do not attack. If people also provide food deliberately through garbage, pet food, carcass waste,

90
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:34,000
campsites, or handouts, the lesson changes again. Human scent stops predicting danger. It begins predicting

91
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:41,000
calories. This is food conditioning and it is one of the clearest pathways from normal weariness to dangerous boldness.

92
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:49,000
A conditioned wolf may approach camps, inspect tents, follow people, or resist attempts to drive it away. If the

93
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:53,000
expected food does not appear, the animal may become increasingly forceful.

94
00:11:53,000 --> 00:12:02,000
The wolf has not become evil. It has been trained badly by people who may never meet the final version of the behavior. Several modern wolf attacks in

95
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:10,000
North America and Europe have involved wolves that had already become unusually fearless around humans, sometimes after receiving food or repeatedly using human

96
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:19,000
areas. The attack was not the first warning. It was the last event in a sequence people had been watching develop. This is why feeding a wolf is

97
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:26,000
not kindness. It is a long-d distanceance decision about who may be bitten later. Young wolves can complicate the picture because curiosity

98
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:34,000
and inexperience may bring them closer than older animals. A juvenile may inspect something unfamiliar, follow for

99
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:41,000
a short distance, or hesitate before retreating. That does not automatically mean it is stalking a human as prey. It

100
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:49,000
does mean the behavior should not be rewarded. Wildlife managers pay attention to wolves that repeatedly approach people, remain nearby despite

101
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:58,000
hazing, enter camps, or appear to seek food. Most close sightings end without injury. But advanced habituation removes

102
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:07,000
one of the main barriers protecting both species. The safest wolf is not the wolf that loves people. It is the wolf that

103
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:16,000
considers people inconvenient and leaves. Rabies removes that barrier in a more violent way. A rabid wolf can

104
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:26,000
behave unlike a healthy one. The disease attacks the nervous system and may produce abnormal aggression, disorientation, impaired movement, loss of fear, and repeated biting.

105
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:35,000
Historically, rabid wolves were responsible for a large share of documented wolf attacks in parts of Europe, the Middle East, and Asia.

106
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:42,000
One infected animal could move through several settlements, biting multiple people and animals during a single episode. Before vaccination and

107
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:50,000
post-exposure treatment, the bite carried another horror. The person might survive the physical injuries only to develop a disease that was nearly always

108
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:59,000
fatal once symptoms appeared. This is one reason old wolf accounts can sound so different from the behavior people observe in many modern protected

109
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:06,000
populations. They came from landscapes with different rabies prevalence, different prey conditions, different waste practices, and different

110
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:14,000
relationships between people, livestock, children, and wild predators. History was not Yellowstone with worse clothing.

111
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:22,000
In a review covering reported attacks from 2002 through 2020, researchers verified 75 cases from the material they

112
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:30,000
could examine. 44 were attributed to rabid wolves, 15 were considered predatory, seven defensive or provoked,

113
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:38,000
and nine remained unclear. The authors warned that their coverage was much stronger in Europe and North America than across parts of Asia. So, the

114
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:45,000
numbers were not a complete global census. The pattern matters more than pretending the database is perfect.

115
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:54,000
attacks cluster around particular conditions. Rabies, loss of fear, food conditioning, scarcity of wild prey,

116
00:14:54,000 --> 00:15:02,000
landscapes where wolves survive on livestock, waste, or food found close to homes, small children alone, or tending

117
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:08,000
animals, people approaching, trapped, injured, cornered, or feeding wolves.

118
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:13,000
The ordinary wild wolf avoiding an adult hiker is not the same situation.

119
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:20,000
Predatory attacks also force an uncomfortable distinction between adults and children. Children are smaller. They

120
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:28,000
may move erratically, run, cry, or remain farther from adults while playing or tending livestock. In regions and historical periods where children worked

121
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:37,000
alone near forest edges or livestock, wolves had more opportunities to encounter a human whose size fell closer to familiar prey. Reviews of verified

122
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:42,000
attacks repeatedly identify children as especially represented in predatory cases.

123
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:50,000
This does not mean wolves possess a category called child. They perceive body size, movement, isolation,

124
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:59,000
resistance, and opportunity. The same logic affects many predators. A healthy adult human standing upright is strange.

125
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:06,000
Confrontational prey connected to possible retaliation. A very small, isolated person reduces several of those

126
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:14,000
uncertainties. Most wolves still do not attack. Risk is not destiny, but the difference helps explain why predatory

127
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:22,000
cases have often involved children and why adults should immediately gather children close during an unusual wolf encounter.

128
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:29,000
Now, consider the pack. Popular culture imagines a wolf pack as a military gang controlled by an alpha that dominates

129
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:35,000
everyone through constant violence. Wild packs are usually much less theatrical.

130
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:42,000
They commonly consist of a breeding pair and offspring of different ages, a family that travels, hunts, raises pups,

131
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:50,000
and defends territory together. This matters because pack cooperation is not automatic confidence. More wolves can

132
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:59,000
help bring down difficult prey such as bison, but every additional animal is still an individual capable of hesitation. Wolves differ in age,

133
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:07,000
experience, condition, and willingness to approach danger. Some lead pursuits, others hang back. Young animals make

134
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:16,000
mistakes. Older ones may recognize risks sooner. A group of wolves is not one brain distributed across several bodies.

135
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:23,000
It is a family attempting to agree while running. When wolves encounter humans, social influence can work in either

136
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:32,000
direction. One curious animal may draw others closer. One alarmed adult may cause the group to retreat. The pack's

137
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:40,000
previous experience with people matters, as does whether they are near pups, a den, or a fresh kill. Defensive behavior

138
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:51,000
is another reason attacks cannot all be explained through hunger. A wolf may bite if trapped, handled, pursued, injured, or prevented from escaping.

139
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:58,000
People have been attacked while trying to rescue dogs, approach dens, interfere with wolves near prey, or capture

140
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:07,000
animals. Livestock conflicts also place humans near wolves during highly aroused situations. The major attack reviews

141
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:13,000
distinguish these defensive and provoked incidents from predatory attacks. A defensive bite does not mean the wolf

142
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:21,000
decided humans were food. Your skin may find the distinction emotionally incomplete. The prevention remains

143
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:28,000
different. Give the animal space. Do not corner it. control dogs and do not approach a wolf because it appears calm

144
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:36,000
enough for a better photograph. Dogs create special tension. To humans, a dog is family, assistant, or enthusiastic

145
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:44,000
roommate. To a wolf, it may be a strange canid entering territory, approaching a den, following scent, barking, or

146
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:51,000
behaving like a competitor. Wolves sometimes attack and kill dogs. The human who intervenes can then be pulled

147
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:58,000
into a conflict that did not begin as predation on people. Dogs can also chase wolves, return toward their owners, and

148
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:07,000
bring the conflict with them. This is one reason leash rules in wolf country are not an insult to the dog's personality. The wolf has not read its

149
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:16,000
profile. There is also a strange contradiction at the center of the human wolf relationship. Dogs came from wolves. At some point, ancient wolves

150
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:23,000
and humans entered a relationship close enough to produce the first domesticated animal. The exact route, location, and

151
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:30,000
number of domestication events remain debated. Some hypotheses involve wolves tolerating human proximity or becoming

152
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:38,000
dependent on resources near people. But researchers still disagree over how the process began. If wolves are so wary of

153
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:45,000
humans, how did that happen? Because wolf behavior varies, a species can contain cautious individuals, curious

154
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:53,000
individuals, bold individuals, and animals whose behavior changes through development and experience.

155
00:19:53,000 --> 00:20:01,000
Domestication did not require every wolf to approach every camp. It required unusual interactions to persist long enough for selection and human choice to

156
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:08,000
reshape later generations. The wolves that became dogs entered an evolutionary arrangement. The wolves that remained

157
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:17,000
wolves continued living beside a species that hunted competitors. Those paths produced animals with very different expectations. A socialized captive wolf

158
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:25,000
is not the same as a wild wolf. A wolf dog hybrid is not a reliable model for wild behavior. And your neighbor's husky looking offended in the snow is not

159
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:34,000
evidence that you understand pack ecology. Wild wolves avoid us partly because they never crossed the final distance dogs did. What about hunger?

160
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:38,000
Surely a starving pack might decide that a person is better than nothing.

161
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:47,000
Possibly extreme food shortage can alter risk-taking and some historical predatory attacks occurred in landscapes with very little wild prey, fragmented

162
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:55,000
habitat, heavy livestock dependence, and human waste. But hunger is not a button that makes every wolf attack the nearest

163
00:20:55,000 --> 00:21:04,000
human. Starving predators can become bolder. They can also become weaker. A human remains unfamiliar and dangerous.

164
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:13,000
Livestock, carrying, garbage, pets, or smaller wild animals may still offer more recognizable options. Even

165
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:21,000
desperate animals make choices based on experience and opportunity. The phrase hungry wolf has done enormous cultural work. Biology remains annoyingly

166
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:27,000
specific. Humans also carry a psychological bias that makes wolf danger feel larger than its probability.

167
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:38,000
A wolf attack is vivid. It resembles an ancient story. It involves darkness, teeth, wilderness, and the unsettling idea that humans can still become meat.

168
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:46,000
The event receives intense attention precisely because it is rare. Meanwhile, ordinary dangers arrive wearing boring

169
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:54,000
clothes, falls, exposure, vehicle crashes, domestic dogs, medical emergencies. The unfamiliar predator

170
00:21:54,000 --> 00:22:03,000
occupies more imagination than the familiar risk because fear is not a spreadsheet. This does not make concern irrational. Wolves are powerful wild

171
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:12,000
animals. A rare event can still be catastrophic to the person experiencing it. The correct response is neither fairy tale panic nor social media

172
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:16,000
confidence that wolves are harmless forest puppies. Respect the animal.

173
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:25,000
Respect the rarity, too. If a wolf appears at normal distance, it will usually move away once it recognizes a person. Do not follow it for a closer

174
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:33,000
view. Do not feed it. Keep dogs controlled and children close. If a wolf approaches or refuses to leave, official guidance advises standing upright,

175
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:42,000
making yourself large, speaking or shouting firmly, backing away rather than running, and using noise or thrown objects to drive it off. Wildlife

176
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:50,000
agencies advise fighting back if an attack occurs. Those actions work with the wolf's normal calculation.

177
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:57,000
You are aware. You are large. You are difficult. You are not providing food.

178
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:08,000
You are increasing the cost of continued approach. The goal is not to prove dominance over the wilderness. It is to help the wolf make the decision most wolves already prefer.

179
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:14,000
Leave. And this is ultimately why wild wolves usually do not attack us. They are capable predators, but they are not

180
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:22,000
careless ones. Their normal prey is familiar, readable, and tested through generations of hunting. Humans are

